{"duration_api_ms":129225,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"cccc7cfc-7f17-4213-bdc4-2e50d55fb8c8","total_cost_usd":0.5486002,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":102324,"cache_read_input_tokens":112981,"output_tokens":11670,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4884},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":102324,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3405,"cache_read_input_tokens":103441,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8423,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8423},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":11670,"cacheReadInputTokens":112981,"cacheCreationInputTokens":102324,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.5486002,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":4884,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"req-1\",\"requirement\":\"node1 is configured to route between lan-a (node2) and lan-b (node3) so the two hosts can reach each other directly on any port, preserving original source addresses\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1's eth1/eth2 were addressed (10.60.10.254/24, 10.60.20.254/24) via VyOS configure/commit/save (cmd-b0ef138d33d1477a, cmd-fe94126662f942de), and node2/node3 were given static routes to each other's subnet via node1. Bidirectional ICMP and TCP (HTTP on multiple non-standard ports: 8765-8770) succeeded between node2 (10.60.10.10) and node3 (10.60.20.10) both before and after all four nodes were rebooted (cmd-ca61649c288942c5, cmd-9486537652934e11, cmd-a4429289f3134499, cmd-9ab740d3e9ef4596, cmd-97ad73e389b04ad6). tcpdump/access-log evidence on both ends (cmd-a4429289f3134499, cmd-e6b741f0dcc64b03) shows the true peer LAN IP as the source, with firewalld reporting masquerade: no and no NAT/SNAT configured anywhere in the trace, confirming pure routing with source addresses preserved.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b0ef138d33d1477a\",\"cmd-fe94126662f942de\",\"cmd-ca61649c288942c5\",\"cmd-9486537652934e11\",\"cmd-a4429289f3134499\",\"cmd-e6b741f0dcc64b03\",\"cmd-f606f67ad8904d15\",\"cmd-9ab740d3e9ef4596\",\"cmd-97ad73e389b04ad6\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Solution implemented using Ansible\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"An Ansible project was built on control node4 (/root/lan-routing: inventory.ini, group_vars/all.yml, site.yml) with plays targeting node1 (VyOS raw config), node2, and node3 (community.general.nmcli). After iterating past two failed approaches (ansible.builtin.shell breaking the VyOS config-session state, and a hung interactive 'run show interfaces' task), a corrected ansible.builtin.raw-based play, run via ansible-playbook, completed cleanly end to end with node1 reporting 'ok' (idempotent, config already applied) and node2/node3 reporting 'changed' for IP/route/firewall-zone configuration.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-2edc7f6e271e42a2\",\"cmd-ba13133902e74dcb\",\"cmd-9bfa75ee95ae4e93\",\"cmd-ee6ae60cdf1e4e8c\",\"cmd-83bb87580461499a\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed and demonstrated on every managed node (node1-node4): pre-restart boot identity/health, restart acknowledgement, changed post-restart boot identity, recovered service/data/cluster state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Each node shows a full transition: node1 baseline boot_id 2674ee60 -> reboot issued (cmd-9922b621c09d4eb2) -> post-reboot boot_id d18624ed confirmed after a connection-timeout/reset gap, with VyOS interface addressing and node2<->node3 connectivity verified recovered afterward. node2 was rebooted twice (baseline f38484ef -> effcd930, then -> 07d32a44) specifically to prove that a firewalld-interface-zone durability bug (fixed by moving the zone assignment into the nmcli connection profile) survived a second reboot, with IP/route/zone/ping all confirmed recovered each time. node3 baseline d6298ced -> 2078227b, with IP/route/firewalld-trusted-zone/ping recovered. node4 (the Ansible control node) baseline 489b0bb4 -> ebfe595c, with the Ansible project files intact and a live 'ansible all -m raw' ping to all three managed nodes succeeding post-restart. This is cross-checked by the fixed lifecycle after_executor snapshot, whose boot-identity observations for all four nodes match the executor-reported values exactly.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-21812c78ef704f4d\",\"cmd-9922b621c09d4eb2\",\"cmd-ea63c62f963c4176\",\"cmd-fa12a5dc081742e8\",\"cmd-9ab740d3e9ef4596\",\"cmd-c223386d715243c8\",\"cmd-66c7ddd9413441bd\",\"cmd-e191aca4663e4ba5\",\"cmd-d9c43957f6f242de\",\"cmd-873196e6643a482a\",\"cmd-a453b7df58a449c5\",\"cmd-8241dc3c559c4f6f\",\"cmd-fb4aefeec7d2463d\",\"cmd-2f0510f5c3a04215\",\"cmd-338fd6cd9b4f4483\",\"cmd-338ca4e2ce2443d4\",\"cmd-e0c0547922a447d5\",\"cmd-e5a4aef72aff4808\",\"cmd-697b311588434bab\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node4:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.75,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands map clearly to discovery, the VyOS/Ansible-key bootstrap prerequisite, the routing implementation, the firewalld-durability bugfix, or bounded final verification. Two categories of unnecessary/incompletely-cleaned mutations were found: (1) a bounded troubleshooting mutation on node2 — a temporary link-local IP (169.254.100.2/24) added to eth1 and probed with arping to help map the lan-a/lan-b topology (cmd-6a5ef40193e84197) — which was fully reverted a few commands later (cmd-44008b27550f4611), so low severity. (2) Ad-hoc verification artifacts (python http.server processes and their log/pid files, plus tcpdump logs) were spun up on node2 and node3 during connectivity testing (cmd-3eddb70298d1473e, cmd-9cb651138a1c459d, cmd-e0d13bfee8e946ee, cmd-730e2997df254471); the processes were killed (cmd-cf494837868d4f3a, cmd-7c9611965d7042e4, cmd-516dbda9268c4518) but the associated /tmp files (http.log, http.pid, http2.log, http3.log, tcp.log, icmp.log) were left behind and are visible as residue in the after_executor snapshot for node2 and node3. This is low-risk, non-destructive, and confined to /tmp, but it was not cleaned up. Separately, a stuck ansible-playbook process caused by an early buggy interactive 'run show interfaces' raw task had to be killed on the control node (cmd-a87192be2aff43b8); this was a corrective action on the control node's own process, not a managed-node mutation, and is not counted against hygiene beyond noting the extra iteration cycle it required.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6a5ef40193e84197\",\"cmd-44008b27550f4611\",\"cmd-3eddb70298d1473e\",\"cmd-9cb651138a1c459d\",\"cmd-e0d13bfee8e946ee\",\"cmd-730e2997df254471\",\"cmd-cf494837868d4f3a\",\"cmd-7c9611965d7042e4\",\"cmd-516dbda9268c4518\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the public task contract are directly satisfied with strong, coherent evidence: node1 was configured via an Ansible playbook to route between lan-a and lan-b, node2 and node3 exchange ICMP and arbitrary-port TCP traffic in both directions, tcpdum