ask), a corrected ansible.builtin.raw-based play, run via ansible-playbook, completed cleanly end to end with node1 reporting 'ok' (idempotent, config already applied) and node2/node3 reporting 'changed' for IP/route/firewall-zone configuration.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2edc7f6e271e42a2","cmd-ba13133902e74dcb","cmd-9bfa75ee95ae4e93","cmd-ee6ae60cdf1e4e8c","cmd-83bb87580461499a"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed and demonstrated on every managed node (node1-node4): pre-restart boot identity/health, restart acknowledgement, changed post-restart boot identity, recovered service/data/cluster state","status":"satisfied","summary":"Each node shows a full transition: node1 baseline boot_id 2674ee60 -> reboot issued (cmd-9922b621c09d4eb2) -> post-reboot boot_id d18624ed confirmed after a connection-timeout/reset gap, with VyOS interface addressing and node2<->node3 connectivity verified recovered afterward. node2 was rebooted twice (baseline f38484ef -> effcd930, then -> 07d32a44) specifically to prove that a firewalld-interface-zone durability bug (fixed by moving the zone assignment into the nmcli connection profile) survived a second reboot, with IP/route/zone/ping all confirmed recovered each time. node3 baseline d6298ced -> 2078227b, with IP/route/firewalld-trusted-zone/ping recovered. node4 (the Ansible control node) baseline 489b0bb4 -> ebfe595c, with the Ansible project files intact and a live 'ansible all -m raw' ping to all three managed nodes succeeding post-restart. This is cross-checked by the fixed lifecycle after_executor snapshot, whose boot-identity observations for all four nodes match the executor-reported values exactly.","evidence_ids":["cmd-21812c78ef704f4d","cmd-9922b621c09d4eb2","cmd-ea63c62f963c4176","cmd-fa12a5dc081742e8","cmd-9ab740d3e9ef4596","cmd-c223386d715243c8","cmd-66c7ddd9413441bd","cmd-e191aca4663e4ba5","cmd-d9c43957f6f242de","cmd-873196e6643a482a","cmd-a453b7df58a449c5","cmd-8241dc3c559c4f6f