ddb70298d1473e","cmd-9cb651138a1c459d","cmd-e0d13bfee8e946ee","cmd-730e2997df254471","cmd-cf494837868d4f3a","cmd-7c9611965d7042e4","cmd-516dbda9268c4518","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes of the public task contract are directly satisfied with strong, coherent evidence: node1 was configured via an Ansible playbook to route between lan-a and lan-b, node2 and node3 exchange ICMP and arbitrary-port TCP traffic in both directions, tcpdump/log evidence confirms original source addresses are preserved (no NAT), and the implementation runs through ansible-playbook after correcting two failed VyOS automation approaches. The independent provider-wide restart-evidence protocol was also completed on all four nodes with clear before/after boot-identity pairs, restart acknowledgements, and confirmed recovery of routing, firewall-zone, and connectivity state, including a deliberate second reboot of node2 to prove a discovered firewalld-durability bug was actually fixed. Hygiene is good overall; the only deductions are a small reverted troubleshooting mutation and a handful of leftover debug log files in /tmp on node2/node3.","limitations":["Evidence for node1's forwarding behavior relies on empirical ping/TCP tests rather than an explicit inspection of a VyOS firewall/forwarding ruleset, though the observed successful bidirectional traffic is direct evidence of correct behavior.","The stuck ansible-playbook process (cmd-6914619a3ef54f6d) timed out server-side before being killed; it is not fully clear from the trace whether it partially mutated node1/node2/node3 state before termination, though subsequent commands show a consistent, correct final state.","Leftover /tmp debug artifacts (http.log/pid, tcp.log, icmp.log) on node2 and node3 were not explicitly confirmed removed in the trace, though they are low-risk and do not affect functional correctness."]},"ttft_ms":63088,"type":"